---
layout: base.njk
# Leads with what someone is searching for rather than with the name, and fits
# in the roughly 60 characters a result actually shows.
title: "What a California house actually costs | Three Blueberries"
description: "The real monthly cost of buying a house in California: property tax, Mello-Roos, insurance, PMI, maintenance, all itemised and cited. No lead capture, no email gate."
---

<header class="hero">
  <p class="hero__kicker">
    <img class="berries" src="/assets/icons/berries.svg" alt="" width="64" height="64" /> Three Blueberries
  </p>
  <h1>What does this house <em>actually</em> cost?</h1>
  <p class="hero__sub">
    Named for the meme where boomers bought a house with a firm handshake and three blueberries. This works out
    what one costs now, at today's rates, with nothing left out.
  </p>
  <p class="hero__sub hero__sub--quiet">
    Every other mortgage calculator is built by someone selling you a loan, and every one of them omits something.
    This shows the whole bill and cites where each number came from.
  </p>
</header>

<form id="scenario" class="panel" data-toc-label="The calculator">
  <div class="grid">
    <fieldset>
      <legend>The house</legend>

      <label for="purchasePrice">Purchase price</label>
      <input id="purchasePrice" type="text" class="money" value="900,000" inputmode="numeric" />

      <label for="county">County</label>
      <select id="county"></select>

      <label for="hoaMonthly">HOA dues <span class="hint">per month</span></label>
      <input id="hoaMonthly" type="text" class="money" value="0" inputmode="numeric" />

      <label for="melloRoosAnnual">
        Mello-Roos <span class="hint">per year, check the title report, it won't be on Zillow</span>
      </label>
      <input id="melloRoosAnnual" type="text" class="money" value="0" inputmode="numeric" />

      <label for="squareFeet">Square feet <span class="hint">optional, used for the VA residual test</span></label>
      <input id="squareFeet" type="text" class="money" placeholder="1,500" inputmode="numeric" />
    </fieldset>

    <fieldset>
      <legend>The loan</legend>

      <label for="loanType">Loan type</label>
      <select id="loanType">
        <option value="conventional" selected>Conventional</option>
        <option value="va">VA</option>
        <option value="fha">FHA</option>
        <option value="jumbo">Jumbo</option>
      </select>

      <label for="downPaymentAmount">
        Down payment <span class="hint" id="downPaymentPercentOut">20.0% of the price</span>
      </label>
      <input id="downPaymentAmount" type="text" class="money" value="180,000" inputmode="numeric" />

      <label for="interestRate">Interest rate <span class="hint">percent</span></label>
      <input id="interestRate" type="number" value="6.66" step="0.005" min="0" inputmode="decimal" />
      <p id="rate-note" class="field-note">Fetching the current national average…</p>

      <label for="termYears">Term</label>
      <select id="termYears">
        <option value="30" selected>30 years</option>
        <option value="15">15 years</option>
      </select>

      <label for="creditScore">Credit score</label>
      <input id="creditScore" type="number" value="740" step="10" min="300" max="850" inputmode="numeric" />

      <div id="va-fields" hidden>
        <label class="check">
          <input id="vaFirstUse" type="checkbox" checked /> First time using a VA loan
        </label>
        <label class="check">
          <input id="vaDisabilityExempt" type="checkbox" />
          Service-connected disability rating <span class="hint">waives the funding fee entirely</span>
        </label>
      </div>
    </fieldset>

    <fieldset>
      <legend>You</legend>

      <label for="income1">Gross annual income</label>
      <input id="income1" type="text" class="money" value="180,000" inputmode="numeric" />

      <label for="income2">Second income <span class="hint">optional</span></label>
      <input id="income2" type="text" class="money" placeholder="none" inputmode="numeric" />

      <label for="monthlyDebts">
        Other monthly debt <span class="hint">car, student loans, card minimums</span>
      </label>
      <input id="monthlyDebts" type="text" class="money" value="750" inputmode="numeric" />

      <label for="householdSize">Household size</label>
      <input id="householdSize" type="number" value="2" step="1" min="1" inputmode="numeric" />

      <label for="currentRent">Rent you pay now <span class="hint">per month</span></label>
      <input id="currentRent" type="text" class="money" value="2,750" inputmode="numeric" />

      <label for="currentSavings">Saved so far</label>
      <input id="currentSavings" type="text" class="money" value="40,000" inputmode="numeric" />

      <label for="monthlySavings">Saving per month</label>
      <input id="monthlySavings" type="text" class="money" value="2,000" inputmode="numeric" />

      <label for="rentalIncome">
        Second unit income <span class="hint">the other half of a duplex, or an existing ADU</span>
      </label>
      <input id="rentalIncome" type="text" class="money" value="0" inputmode="numeric" />

      <label class="check">
        <input id="excludeRental" type="checkbox" />
        I would not let out part of a home
        <span class="hint">removes it as a path to yes</span>
      </label>
    </fieldset>

  </div>

  <details class="overrides">
    <summary>Overrides <span class="hint">leave these blank to use our estimates</span></summary>
    <div class="grid">
      <fieldset>

      <label for="insuranceAnnual">
        Homeowners insurance <span class="hint">per year. Use a real quote if you have one</span>
      </label>
      <input id="insuranceAnnual" type="text" class="money" placeholder="estimated" inputmode="numeric" />

      <label for="propertyTaxRate">
        Property tax rate <span class="hint">percent, from your county assessor</span>
      </label>
      <input id="propertyTaxRate" type="number" placeholder="county default" step="0.01" min="0" inputmode="decimal" />

      <label class="check">
        <input id="homeownersExemption" type="checkbox" checked />
        Claiming the homeowners' exemption <span class="hint">$7,000 off assessed value. You must file</span>
      </label>
      </fieldset>
    </div>
  </details>
</form>

<section class="panel">
  <h2>What will it actually cost?</h2>
  <p class="panel__sub">Open a line to see how it was calculated.</p>
  <div id="breakdown" class="breakdown"></div>
</section>

<section class="panel panel--warn">
  <h2>Things nobody tells you</h2>
  {#
    Seeded with the one warning that fires on every scenario, so this section is
    not an empty list to anything without JavaScript, which includes a crawler
    landing on the page cold. The script replaces the list on first render with
    the full set for the numbers actually entered. Nothing here is a claim that
    depends on the form, so nothing here can be wrong.
  #}
  <ul id="warnings" class="warnings">
    <li>
      Expect a supplemental property tax bill a few months after closing. When the county reassesses to your
      purchase price, it bills you the difference from the seller's old assessment, prorated for the rest of the
      fiscal year. Your impound account does not cover it, it arrives as a separate bill you pay out of pocket,
      and on a long-held California home it can be thousands.
    </li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="panel">
  <h2>Can you buy it?</h2>
  <div id="qualification" class="stats"></div>
  <ul id="notes" class="notes"></ul>
</section>

<section class="panel">
  <h2>Should you buy it?</h2>

  {#
    The answer goes FIRST, before any of the reasoning. This section used to
    open by refusing to answer ("Not a yes or no: that depends on assumptions
    nobody can pin down"), which is true of the rent-versus-buy half and false
    of the two gates that actually stop most purchases: whether a lender will
    lend, and whether the cash is there. Both are arithmetic. Hedging all three
    because the third is uncertain handed the reader a shrug.
  #}
  <div id="recommendation" class="recommendation"></div>

  <p class="panel__sub">
    "Rent is throwing money away" is the argument everyone makes, and it is mostly wrong. The honest version:
    net worth under both paths, with the renter investing what the buyer sank into the house.
  </p>

  <div id="rentRatio" class="stats"></div>

  <h3 class="sub-head">What would have to change</h3>
  <p class="panel__sub">
    The answer above turns on one binding constraint. These are the others, each stated as the number it would
    have to reach. Usually one is much closer than the rest.
  </p>
  <h4 class="chart-title">What the interest rate actually decides</h4>
  <div id="buyZoneChart"></div>
  <p id="buyZoneNote" class="field-note"></p>

  <p id="decisionVerdict" class="verdict"></p>
  <div id="decisionLevers" class="levers"></div>


  <h3 class="sub-head">Year one, where the money actually goes</h3>
  <div id="burnCompare" class="stats"></div>

  <h3 class="sub-head">Net worth, both paths</h3>
  <div id="rentBuyChart"></div>
  <p id="rentBuyVerdict" class="verdict"></p>

  <p class="panel__sub">
    Appreciation and investment return have to come from the same period, or the comparison is rigged without
    anyone meaning to rig it. These set both together.
  </p>
  <div id="assumptionSets" class="presets"></div>
  <p id="assumptionBasis" class="field-note"></p>

  <div class="grid grid--tight">
    <div>
      <label for="appreciation">Home appreciation <output id="appreciationOut">6.5%</output>/yr</label>
      <!-- Goes NEGATIVE on purpose. Butte's last twenty years run at -1.4%/yr, and
           a floor of zero would have clamped that preset silently to 0% while the
           caption underneath said -1.4%. -->
      <input id="appreciation" type="range" min="-30" max="90" value="65" step="1" />
    </div>
    <div>
      <label for="investReturn">
        Return on invested savings <output id="investReturnOut">10.0%</output>/yr
        <span class="hint">what the money you did not spend on the house earns</span>
      </label>
      <!-- Tops out above the highest named set (14.7%), which the old 12% cap
           silently clamped, so the caption said one thing and the panel ran another. -->
      <input id="investReturn" type="range" min="0" max="150" value="100" step="1" />
      <label for="rentGrowth">Rent rises <output id="rentGrowthOut">3.5%</output>/yr</label>
      <input id="rentGrowth" type="range" min="0" max="80" value="35" step="1" />
    </div>
  </div>
  <p id="rentBuyCaveat" class="field-note"></p>

  <h3 class="sub-head">How long would you have to stay?</h3>
  <p class="panel__sub">
    Buying has large costs at both ends: closing fees going in, commission going out, and years of payments that
    are almost entirely interest. Sell before the crossover and you would have been wealthier renting.
  </p>
  <label for="holdYears" class="slider-label">
    You expect to stay <output id="holdYearsOut">10 years</output>
  </label>
  <input id="holdYears" type="range" min="2" max="30" value="10" step="1" />
  <div id="holdVerdict" class="stats"></div>
  <div id="breakevenChart"></div>
  <p id="rentCapNote" class="field-note"></p>

  <h3 class="sub-head">How much do you need saved, and are you gaining on it?</h3>
  <div id="savingsRace" class="stats"></div>
  <p id="savingsVerdict" class="verdict"></p>
</section>

<section class="panel">
  <h2>What if you wait?</h2>
  <p class="panel__sub">
    Decades of prices in your county, and the rate that went with them. Top chart is what a house cost; bottom is
    what it cost <em>per month</em>. They're not the same shape, that's the argument.
  </p>
  <p class="scope-note" data-county-scope></p>

  <div id="history" class="history"></div>

  <h3 class="sub-head">Is this the worst time in history to buy?</h3>
  <p id="worstTime" class="verdict"></p>

  <h3 class="sub-head">Is there a tell?</h3>
  <p class="panel__sub">
    Today's readings against your county's own bubble peak and the bottom that followed. A handful of declines
    across one county's record isn't a sample you can forecast from: this describes conditions, it doesn't predict.
    The caveats below say how many your county actually has.
  </p>
  <div id="signals" class="signals"></div>
  <p id="signalSummary" class="verdict"></p>
  <details class="method">
    <summary>How well does any of this actually correlate?</summary>
    <div id="correlations"></div>
    <ul id="signalCaveats" class="warnings"></ul>
  </details>

  <h3 class="sub-head">We trained a forecaster. It doesn't work.</h3>
  <p class="panel__sub">
    A real one: three model families, 20 metros, validated walk-forward. It is trained on Case-Shiller and
    produces a number for San Diego only, so it does not follow the county selector and does not match the FHFA
    charts above. That number sits <em>below</em> its own track record, because the track record is the point.
  </p>
  <div id="forecastVerdict"></div>
  <div id="forecastCurrent" class="stats"></div>
  <details class="method">
    <summary>What it learned to weight, and how it scored</summary>
    <div id="forecastWeights"></div>
    <div id="forecastScores"></div>
  </details>

  <h3 class="sub-head">What the next one might be built out of</h3>
  <p id="watchPreamble" class="panel__sub"></p>
  <div id="instruments" class="instruments"></div>
  <p id="watchDiscipline" class="field-note"></p>

  <h3 class="sub-head">Model your own crash</h3>
  <p class="panel__sub">
    The first two presets are your county's own declines, at the depth and duration they actually ran. The
    default is the shallower of them: averaging a shallow decline and a severe one invents a "typical crash"
    that never happened anywhere. The rest are scenarios, not history, and each one says which it is.
  </p>
  <div id="presets" class="presets"></div>
  <p id="presetBasis" class="field-note"></p>
  <div class="grid grid--tight">
    <div>
      <label for="crashDepth">Prices fall <output id="crashDepthOut">25%</output></label>
      <input id="crashDepth" type="range" min="0" max="50" value="25" step="1" />

      <label for="crashMonths">over <output id="crashMonthsOut">36 months</output></label>
      <input id="crashMonths" type="range" min="6" max="84" value="36" step="1" />
    </div>
    <div>
      <label for="crashRate">Rate at the bottom: <output id="crashRateOut">6.00%</output></label>
      <input id="crashRate" type="range" min="300" max="1100" value="600" step="5" />

      <label for="waitRent">Your rent while waiting <span class="hint">follows the form above until you change it</span></label>
      <input id="waitRent" type="text" class="money" value="2,750" inputmode="numeric" />

      <label for="waitSavings">Extra saved per month <span class="hint">goes to the down payment</span></label>
      <input id="waitSavings" type="text" class="money" value="1,000" inputmode="numeric" />
    </div>
  </div>

  <div id="waiting" class="stats"></div>
  <p id="waitingVerdict" class="verdict"></p>
  <ul id="waitingCaveats" class="warnings"></ul>
</section>

<section class="panel">
  <h2>Why does everyone else seem to manage?</h2>
  <p class="panel__sub">
    Almost never because they out-earn you. Here's what the person already living in this house pays.
  </p>
  <p class="scope-note" data-county-scope></p>

  <label for="cohortYear" class="slider-label">
    They bought in <output id="cohortYearOut">2019</output>
    <span class="hint">drag or click a bar</span>
  </label>
  <input id="cohortYear" type="range" min="1990" max="2025" value="2019" step="1" />

  <div id="cohortChart" class="cohort-chart"></div>
  <div id="cohort" class="stats"></div>
  <ul id="cohortNotes" class="notes"></ul>
</section>

<section class="panel panel--thesis">
  <h2>Why this is so hard</h2>
  <p class="panel__sub">
    The three blueberries, finally. Prices rose, but so did everything else, so a price on its own proves
    nothing. What matters is what the <em>same job</em> buys. Below: what the same house cost in your county,
    against what a median CALIFORNIA income could carry at 30% of pay. Statewide income, local prices: the
    panel after this one uses each county's own income instead, and answers a different question.
  </p>
  <p class="scope-note" data-county-scope></p>
  <label class="check">
    <input id="realDollars" type="checkbox" checked />
    Show every year in today's money
    <span class="hint">
      Old dollars were worth more. Nominal prices make everything look like it exploded; the ratio between the two
      lines is the same either way.
    </span>
  </label>
  <div id="buyingPowerChart"></div>
  <p id="buyingPowerHeadline" class="verdict"></p>
  <div id="buyingPower" class="stats"></div>
  <p id="buyingPowerCaveat" class="field-note"></p>

  <h3 class="sub-head">So where does the arithmetic still work?</h3>
  <p class="panel__sub">
    Measured county by county against what people <em>there</em> actually earn, not a statewide figure. This is a
    snapshot of where things stand, not which way they are moving.
  </p>
  <p id="payTrapHeadline" class="verdict"></p>
  <div id="payTrap" class="stats"></div>
  <div id="countyLadder" class="corr"></div>
  <p id="whereItWorksCaveat" class="field-note"></p>
</section>

